1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:03,680
The Moon is breaking up with us. Slowly.

2
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:10,850
Every year, it drifts 3.8 cm further away. About as fast as your fingernails grow.

3
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:16,350
We know because astronauts left mirrors up there. We still bounce lasers off them.

4
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:24,300
And honestly? It's our fault. Earth spins faster than the Moon circles us, so the ocean bulge it pulls on gets dragged ahead,

5
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:29,450
and that bulge tugs the Moon forward, flinging it a little higher every orbit.

6
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:33,500
The Moon pulls back, too. It's putting the brakes on Earth's spin.

7
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:41,620
When the Moon was young, it was so close it looked more than 10 times bigger in the sky, and a day lasted less than 10 hours.

8
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:49,450
When dinosaurs were around, a day was 23.5 hours, and a year had 372 of them.

9
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:56,120
In about 600 million years, it'll look too small to cover the Sun. No more total eclipses.

10
00:00:56,150 --> 00:01:01,720
But it will never fully leave. The Sun will grow old and swallow the whole drama first.

11
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:08,020
So enjoy the eclipses. You live in the short time when the Moon fits the Sun perfectly.
